  v122 with EXACT weights reached cos 0.9768 at |D|=4. The |D|=4 row here is
  the same thing with the weights measured, so the drop is the cost of not
  cheating. The |D| column is the data axis's actual scaling: the w rms
  column is what sqrt(|D|/S) predicts and is the like-for-like comparison.
  The w max column is a max over |D| draws, runs higher, and is the number
  that decides whether the WORST sample's weight flips sign - which is what
  actually breaks the two-branch positive/negative split.

  Circuits stay at 3 per epoch throughout - flat in |D| AND in M, since a QML
  readout is one Pauli so G=1. That is the claim; the cos column is whether
  it is worth anything.

  Scope: N_sys=3, one dataset per |D|, one R, 32768 shots, no noise model, no
  seed averaging, |D| only to 16. The per-sample gradients gs are exact - the
  V6 estimate enters through g_stack alone.
